# Glance Card

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

A glance card shows the state of several things in one row, so a person can see at a glance whether the front door is locked, the hall light is on and the kitchen is warm. It is for reading, not for control. For switches and sliders use a Tile or Entities card.

This page covers:

- Glance Card
  - `hui-glance-card`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

The card has an optional title and one cell for each entity. A cell shows the name above, a small icon in the middle and the state below, all centred. A cell can show the time since the state last changed instead of the state. If an entity cannot be found, its cell shows a warning tint with a message in place of a state.

Cells share the width equally. Text on one line is cut short with an ellipsis, so a long name or state loses its end.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-hui-glance-card"><code>hui-glance-card</code></h3>

The glance card (`type: glance`).

```yaml
type: glance
title: Ground floor
columns: 4
entities:
  - entity: lock.front_door
    name: Front door
  - entity: light.hall
    name: Hall light
  - entity: sensor.kitchen_temperature
    name: Kitchen
  - entity: cover.garage_door
    name: Garage
```

- `entities` is required. Each item is an entity name or an object with `entity`, `name`, `icon`, `image` and `show_last_changed`. Set `show_last_changed: true` on an entity to show the time since its state changed.
- `title` sets the card heading, drawn in the Civica card heading style.
- `show_name`, `show_state` and `show_icon` turn parts of the cells off. Keep the name and the state on.
- `columns` sets how many cells sit in a row. The default is the number of entities, up to 5.
- `tap_action`, `hold_action` and `double_tap_action` set what a cell does. Tapping opens More info by default. An action that calls a service must name the service, or the card shows an error.
- `color` sets how the icon is coloured. By default it follows the state.

The card uses the theme’s card heading colour, size and font, and the primary text colour. A missing entity is tinted with `warning-color` (#8A6100 in light mode, #DED3B8 in dark mode).

<h3 id="focus">Keyboard focus</h3>

A cell with an action can be reached with the keyboard. Home Assistant shows focus by tinting the cell’s background with `divider-color` (#CECECE in light mode, #50565A in dark mode) and rounding its corners. It draws no outline. The Civica theme adds an outline and yellow ring to the card as a whole, but not to the cells inside it. With card-mod, which needs the card-mod integration, you can add one to the cells:

```yaml
type: glance
card_mod:
  style: |
    .entity:focus {
      outline: 3px solid var(--civica-focus-text);
      outline-offset: 2px;
      box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px var(--ha-color-focus);
    }
entities:
  - lock.front_door
```

A cell is a plain block, not a button, and it has no button role of its own. A cell cannot be given a name that includes its state. If people need to operate the entity, use a Tile or Entities card, or write the same names and states in text.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- Many entities fit in a small space.
- Each cell can open More info or run an action.
- Long names and states are cut short.
- The icon is small and the state is a few characters, so a mix of colour and icon alone is not enough. Keep the state in words.
- The focus tint is a background change with no outline, which can be hard to see.
- The height of a cell depends on its content, so a tap target may be small.
- More than 5 columns make cells too narrow to read.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Use a glance card for a handful of related states in one place. Keep names short, show the state in words, and use no more than 5 columns. Put controls on a Tile or Entities card. For a longer list of names and values, use a [summary list](summary-list.html). For the card surface and its heading, see [Card (Base Surface)](cards.html) and [Card & Section Header](card-and-section-header.html).

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.4.1 Use of Colour and 1.1.1 Non-text Content: say the state in words, because the icon and its colour are not enough.
- 1.4.4 Resize Text and 1.4.10 Reflow: cut-short text hides words at large sizes and narrow screens.
- 2.4.7 Focus Visible and 2.4.11 Focus Not Obscured: the default focus is a background tint. Check it in your own theme, or add the outline above.
- 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum): the size of a cell depends on its content. Test it in your own dashboard.
- 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value: a cell has no role, so do not rely on it as a button.

